<Base
  title="Legal & Disclaimers"
  description="The disclaimers, disclosures, and fine print for this site — written so an actual human can read them."
>
  <span class="pill">The fine print, legible</span>
  <h1>Legal &amp; disclaimers</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Most legal pages are written to be scrolled past. This one is written to be read.
    The jokes are free; the disclaimers are binding.
  </p>

  <h2>Opinion and commentary</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      This is a commentary site. The show argues. That is the product.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unless something is explicitly presented as a sourced fact — with a citation, the
      way the <a href="/statistics">statistics page</a> does it — statements on this site
      and on the show are <strong>opinion, analysis, rhetorical hyperbole, or satire</strong>.
      "This policy is insane" is a viewpoint, not a psychiatric diagnosis. Please
      calibrate accordingly.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Guests, debate opponents, and commenters speak for themselves. Hosting an argument
      is not endorsing both halves of it — that would be logically impressive and
      practically impossible.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Not legal advice</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      Nothing here is legal advice, and reading this site does not create an
      attorney-client relationship — mostly because nobody here is your attorney.
    </p>
    <p>
      Family court is jurisdiction-specific, deadline-driven, and unforgiving of
      guesswork. If you are in a custody or divorce case, talk to a licensed attorney
      in your state. The <a href="/resources">resources page</a> lists places to find
      one, including free legal aid directories.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Not financial or tax advice</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      Nothing here is investment, financial, or tax advice. Contributions to The
      Audacity Network go to <strong>JustPearlyThings, LLC</strong> — a company, not a
      charity — so <strong>do not deduct them on your taxes</strong> unless the checkout
      page one day identifies a qualified 501(c)(3) and hands you a receipt. The IRS
      has no documented sense of humor, and we are not going to test that for you.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Full details on where money goes: <a href="/transparency">transparency page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Not medical or mental-health advice</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      This site discusses heavy topics, including suicide statistics. Discussing data
      is not treatment, and a podcast is not a clinician.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>If you or someone you know is struggling:</strong> in the US, call or text
      <strong>988</strong> (Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline), any hour, any day. That one
      is not a joke, and it never will be.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Accuracy and corrections</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      Published statistics link to primary sources and name the exact table, year, and
      limitation. If we got a number wrong, we would rather fix it than defend it —
      being corrected is cheaper than being wrong twice.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Spotted an error? Email
      <a href="mailto:production@theaudacitynetwork.com">production@theaudacitynetwork.com</a>
      with the page and the source that contradicts it. Receipts beat vibes here, too.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Third-party links</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      This site links out — to YouTube, ticketing pages, government datasets, other
      creators. We do not control those sites, their prices, their availability, or
      their comment sections (nobody controls comment sections). A link is a pointer,
      not a partnership, and outbound links are not paid placements unless a page says
      so explicitly.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Copyright and fair use</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      Original content on this site belongs to JustPearlyThings, LLC. Clips of
      third-party material used in commentary rely on fair use — criticism, comment,
      and news reporting under 17 U.S.C. § 107. Quoting a claim in order to argue with
      it is roughly the oldest genre in human history.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Rights holder with a concern? Email
      <a href="mailto:production@theaudacitynetwork.com">production@theaudacitynetwork.com</a>
      with the specific URL and the material at issue. Actual notices get actual answers.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>
      This is a static site. It has no login, no tracking pixels, no ad network, and no
      cookie banner because there is nothing to consent to. We could not sell your data
      if we wanted to — we do not have any.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Embedded players (like YouTube) and linked services set their own cookies under
      their own policies once you interact with them. That part of the internet is
      between you and them.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="notice" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
    <strong>The unfunny footnote.</strong> This page is a plain-language summary of the
    site's disclaimers, not a substitute for formal Terms of Service or a Privacy Policy
